Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Only free location.reference when the location holds a reference (fpc 0.99)
License: expat

tlocation is a variant record.  The `reference : treference' field
belongs to the LOC_MEM/LOC_REFERENCE arm -- it aliases the same
storage as the register/resflags arms used by LOC_REGISTER,
LOC_FLAGS, LOC_FPU, LOC_JUMP, and so on.

compiler/cg386cnv.pas's second_bool_to_int and second_int_to_bool
both call:

    del_reference(pfrom^.location.reference);

without first checking `pfrom^.location.loc'.  When pfrom's location
is a register (or flags, or ...), `location.reference' aliases
whatever bytes the other variant wrote there; `del_reference' then
reads `ref.is_immediate' (a boolean) from those bytes and hands
`ref.base' / `ref.index' -- which at that point are a mix of the
register's ordinal and uninitialised padding -- to
`ungetregister32'.  Native FPC tolerated this because the wrong
"deallocations" usually targeted registers that were already unused
or already held the same register number, so no state went visibly
wrong.  Under tp2cc the boolean read is checked at runtime and the
out-of-range value (the register ordinal recycled into the
is_immediate slot) is flagged at p_tgeni386.cc:195.

Fix: guard both call sites with
`pfrom^.location.loc in [LOC_REFERENCE,LOC_MEM]', matching the test
del_locref already does internally.  Other del_reference call sites
elsewhere in the tree sit in case arms that have already established
loc=LOC_MEM/LOC_REFERENCE; only these two were unguarded.

--- a/compiler/cg386cnv.pas
+++ b/compiler/cg386cnv.pas
@@ -1050,7 +1050,13 @@
            end;
          clear_location(pto^.location);
          pto^.location.loc:=LOC_REGISTER;
-         del_reference(pfrom^.location.reference);
+         { `location.reference' is the LOC_MEM/LOC_REFERENCE arm of
+           tlocation's variant part; reading it when loc is a register
+           variant (LOC_REGISTER, LOC_FLAGS, ...) reads bytes belonging to
+           a different variant and was just wrong.  Only free the
+           reference when the location really holds one. }
+         if pfrom^.location.loc in [LOC_REFERENCE,LOC_MEM] then
+           del_reference(pfrom^.location.reference);
          case pfrom^.resulttype^.size of
           1 : begin
                 case pto^.resulttype^.size of
@@ -1160,7 +1166,11 @@
               exit;
            end;
          pto^.location.loc:=LOC_REGISTER;
-         del_reference(pfrom^.location.reference);
+         { Only free the reference if the location actually holds one;
+           otherwise `location.reference' aliases a different variant
+           (register slot, resflags, ...) and the read is meaningless. }
+         if pfrom^.location.loc in [LOC_REFERENCE,LOC_MEM] then
+           del_reference(pfrom^.location.reference);
          opsize:=def_opsize(pfrom^.resulttype);
          case pfrom^.location.loc of
             LOC_MEM,LOC_REFERENCE :
